Gap: `lanes::permute` picked by `cfg(target_arch)`, and `Lane`'s default `absorb` and `digests` - the extraction would differ by host, and Aeneas translates neither default body, which leaves `impl Lane for u64` without a usable instance
Three changes, so that the permutation extracts for the one lane type every host has, `u64`.

(a) One `permute` on every host. The source has two: x86-64's alternates between two states, a row
at a time, and its `permute_first_row` computes only the first row in the last round; every other
architecture's works in place. The patch gives every host the second, so on x86-64 the extracted
body is not the one a build of the source runs there. x86-64's `permute`, `permute_first_row` and
`round` stay, for tests only, as `permute_two_states` and `permute_first_row_two_states`.
(b) `Lane::absorb` and `Lane::digests`, which only batched hashing calls, move to a trait `Batch:
Lane` with no default bodies. The defaults become `absorb_by_lane` and `digests_by_lane`, which
`u64` and `Avx2` delegate to; `Avx512`'s and `Neon`'s own versions move to their `Batch` impls
unchanged. `hash_many_with` and `hash_group` ask for `Batch`. Nothing but the trait an item sits in
changes. Tool bug: https://github.com/pereq-dev/hax/issues/2404.
(c) `theta`'s `d.iter_mut().enumerate()` and `permute`'s `for &rc in &RC` go by index.

Tests: the_permutation_matches_the_source_on_every_backend (none has a branch on its input), on
`u64` and on each vector backend the CPU runs, from the zero state, the all-ones state and 16
states whose lanes all differ: `permute` against `permute_two_states` and against the in-place
form with its iterators, copied from the source; lanes `0..5` of `permute_first_row` against
`permute_first_row_two_states`; `theta` against its iterator form, copied. Run on aarch64 and,
with `--target x86_64-apple-darwin`, on x86-64 under Rosetta, where the CPU reports no AVX2, so
that run compared `u64` alone. The oracles are `cfg(test)` on every host, being generic code, so
the comparison runs wherever the tests do. They share `theta`, `pi_row` and `chi_row` with
`permute` and say nothing about those: `theta` has its own comparison, and the whole permutation
is checked by matches_ethereum_keccak256, against the `sha3` crate.
the_scalar_batch_matches_one_shot: `hash_many_with::<u64>`, which is `Batch for u64`, against
`hash_from_state` on each input. every_backend_matches_the_scalar_one and
batched_hash_matches_one_shot, the source's own, cover the vector backends' `Batch`.
mutation: `permute` one round short (`0..RC.len() - 1`) -> the_permutation_.. fails, on aarch64 and
on x86-64; round constants reversed (`RC[RC.len() - 1 - round]`) -> it fails; `theta` one column
short (`0..4`) -> it fails, at the `theta` comparison; `Batch for u64`'s `absorb` one word short ->
the_scalar_batch_matches_one_shot and every_backend_matches_the_scalar_one fail.

